e for a lot of the  unpleasant hangover symptoms  so yeah your girl gweny is definitely on  something when it comes to  the egg sandwiches however you know i'm  gonna say something about the iv  therapy recommendation i mean of course  this is something she would suggest i'm  pretty sure  goop invented iv therapy bars but in  short  proponents of iv vitamin therapy claim  that it helps to rid the body of toxins  balance hormones make skin glow boosts  the immune system  and yes of course cure hangovers  they also suggest that it is more  effective than oral vitamins because it  gets into your system faster by going  straight into your bloodstream  through injection now i gotta say that's  not necessarily a good thing  because in that you're bypassing the  digestive tract  which houses the body's built-in  safeguards and filters  that really do help to keep us healthy  and safe and considering there is no  real research to back up the efficacy of  iv  vitamin therapy and it isn't even fda  approved  i would feel a little weary letting  somebody who isn't even a doctor dump a  bag of vitamins  into my veins i know that vitamins and  minerals are  natural substances but they're actually  not benign  there's also very little evidence on how  an iv infusion  may compare to getting your vitamins in  capsule form however unless you're  actually deficient you likely don't need  to get it into your system  that much faster and if there is a  benefit to it for the otherwise healthy  population  it's actually likely largely the placebo  effect  for example one study found that both  the iv therapy group  and the placebo group felt better after  they had the  treatment when compared to baseline i  mean  i would want to believe that i was doing  something too if i was spending 100  or more per poke now despite no real  evidence to back up  its claims and the hefty price tag iv  vitamin therapy  also comes with its own set of risks  like bruising  inflammation of the vein and bleeding we  also need to consider that taking an  excess of vitamins  is not necessarily better and can have  adverse effects if administered beyond  their tolerable limits  so for example getting too much vitamin  a could put one at risk of liver damage  it's also important to note that those  with a history of kidney disease  high blood pressure or heart disease  should especially avoid iv  vitamin therapy as they are already at  risk of dangerous fluid overload  so yeah if you are generally healthy and  i get the sense that gwyneth is in  pretty good health  you're better off sticking with your  oral multivitamins  hydrating and eating a nutritious meal  after a big night on the town  or just do as i do and like go to bed at  8pm because  like gwen says in this video i'm too old  for that crap  next i'm usually eating for lunch stuff  that you would see on the goop website  like  a salad with some protein or you know  they're using jicama for taco shells  you know they'll do like a really clean  version of a turkey burger  oh yeah i was waiting to hear the words  clean or detox in this interview  somewhere  she didn't disappoint yeah  anyways so gwyneth mentions a few  examples of  clean goop approved meals that she eats  like a taco made with jicama instead of  a tortilla or a burger made with a  lettuce bun  so after her morning ritual and her  smoothie  i'm kind of getting the sense that  gwyneth likes to keep things pretty low  carb and hey like i've always said if  eating less carbs  feels good to you amazing but can we  please just throw  clean and detox out of our vocabularies  already  and start describing food for what it  really is  like maybe that jicama is super crunchy  or the lettuce is refreshing  and that's what she truly craves can we  focus on its  absolute true virtues not the assumption  that abstaining from some white carb  element  makes it an inherently cleaner choice  now obviously both of these do sound  like tasty nutritious meals as does 