ng kids  so contrary to the lower carb meals  earlier on in the day  we are getting a lot more balance with  this example of a meal so we've got some  protein the chicken she mentioned  and some fiber in the starchy root  vegetables or potatoes  so yay for more flexibility and balance  we try to eat early it depends on the  day but i like to eat dinner on the  early side i always feel  like i don't sleep very well if i go to  sleep very full so  i've gotten real geriatric about that  like  6 6 30 dinner time it feels good  so there is actually some research to  suggest that eating close to bedtime  can shift sleep patterns and delay the  onset of sleep  some studies have also shown that  consuming high glycemic index  carbohydrates in particular  may make it more difficult for us to  fall asleep quickly  however the same can be said for going  to bed hungry  as hunger pains can keep the brain alert  so it can be a bit of a delicate balance  so that's not to say that you can't eat  after 8 pm or whatever bogus diet rule  diet culture tells us these days  but you may have to experiment with your  own personal routine  to find a meal time and a bedtime that  best works for you so they're just a few  things  that i won't eat i  don't like dill i can't stand dill i  don't like to eat  baby mammals so i stay away from like  lamb and veal and  i don't eat octopus because they're  super smart  we don't agree on a lot of things  gwyneth but i definitely agree i don't  like dill  it sounds like she's pretty open to  eating a variety of types of foods  without any specific dietary  restrictions  the foods that she does avoid are things  like dill and lamb  veal and octopus are either due to a  taste preference  or ethical concerns rather than a belief  about them being  you know toxic or bad which is kind of  off-brand for her  but it does feel pretty refreshing to  hear and i hope that that's actually  true  cheat meal oh it's hard to name just  one cheat meal i love french fries which  is a well-known fact  and i love i do love pasta i really do  uh yes gotta chat about that cheat meal  and i get that this is a standard  harper's bizarre question but  i am waiting for an influencer to call  that out as bs and just say  hey nope no such thing as a cheat meal  anyways  harper's bazaar if you want to interview  me that's going to be my answer just so  you know  but anyway when it comes to gwyneth's  cheat meals gweny is apparently down  for all the carbs i mean namely fries  and pasta  is butter a carb i'm honestly not  surprised that she would exclusively  want to eat straight up carbs  considering they seem to be pretty  lacking in her day to day that's what  happens when we restrict folks  we want to eat all the things  i also find it pretty interesting that  gwyneth considers pasta to be  a cheat meal since there's tons of pasta  recipes on the goop website  and she often has aligned herself  professionally  with the infamous american chef mario  batali who specializes in  italian cuisine i guess i just find it  problematic when social media  influencers and celebrities  present themselves as having this fun  carefree healthy relationship with food  by aligning themselves with foodies and  chefs who are obviously  anti-diet culture and then claiming to  eat without explicit food rules  but then they turn around and capitalize  on an entire  brand focused around pseudoscience diet  rules  clean eating and privileged wellness  culture  oh that's just gwyneth who does that  right anyways in conclusion  while we don't have any information on  exact portion sizes  or what granite state a day actually  looks like when it comes to eating  at first blush this doesn't really seem  like a lot of food for a  busy and active business women like  gwyneth  we're mostly seeing a lot of liquids  lighter  low-carb meals supplements of course and  only  one really substantial and balanced meal  at the end of the day with her family  and who knows how m